From a2ed3e8199414f6f6984e02f1c4fbaee60512298 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:02:52 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Offer a 16-bit canvas surface and use the deepest one by
 default

The canvas surface was limited to 8 or 10 bits per channel in the
non-HDR, surface-managed path (KRITA_USE_SURFACE_COLOR_MANAGEMENT_API),
although Mesa's EGL on Wayland also offers 16-bit window surfaces
(ABGR16161616 and the half-float formats) and Qt's Wayland platform
hands them out when asked for 16 bits per channel. A deep-colour
display chain (HDMI 10, 12 and 16 bits per channel) then gets a canvas
that is quantized to 10 bits before the compositor sees it.

Add a "16-bit" canvas surface bit depth mode next to "8-bit" and
"10-bit". "Auto" now requests the deepest surface: the candidates are
probed 16, 10, 8 bits in turn (the first one the stack creates wins) and
an explicit setting is honoured first, then the deepest depth below it.
The canvas widget uses a GL_RGBA16 texture with the 16-bit surface
(GL_RGBA16F on OpenGL ES, where normalized 16-bit textures are not
core), the preferences dialog lists the new mode and shows the current
depth.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
